.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/eslint-plugin-react-hooks Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "`eslint-plugin-react-hooks`"
.PP
This ESLint plugin enforces the \fRRules of Hooks\fP\&.

.PP
It is a part of the \fRHooks API\fP for React\&.
.SH "Installation"
.PP
\fBNote: If you're using Create React App, please use \fRreact-scripts\fP >= 3 instead of adding it directly\&.\fP

.PP
Assuming you already have ESLint installed, run:

.PP
.PP
.nf
# npm
npm install eslint\-plugin\-react\-hooks \-\-save\-dev

# yarn
yarn add eslint\-plugin\-react\-hooks \-\-dev
.fi
.PP
.SS "Legacy Config (\&.eslintrc)"
If you are still using ESLint below 9\&.0\&.0, please continue to use \fRrecommended-legacy\fP\&. To avoid breaking changes, we still support \fRrecommended\fP as well, but note that this will be changed to alias the flat recommended config in v6\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
{
  "extends": [
    // \&.\&.\&.
    "plugin:react\-hooks/recommended\-legacy"
  ]
}
.fi
.PP
.SS "Flat Config (eslint\&.config\&.js)"
For \fRESLint 9\&.0\&.0 and above\fP users, add the \fRrecommended-latest\fP config\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
import reactHooks from 'eslint\-plugin\-react\-hooks';

export default [
  // \&.\&.\&.
  reactHooks\&.configs['recommended\-latest'],
];
.fi
.PP
.SS "Custom Configuration"
If you want more fine-grained configuration, you can instead add a snippet like this to your ESLint configuration file:
.SS "Legacy Config (\&.eslintrc)"
.PP
.nf
{
  "plugins": [
    // \&.\&.\&.
    "react\-hooks"
  ],
  "rules": {
    // \&.\&.\&.
    "react\-hooks/rules\-of\-hooks": "error",
    "react\-hooks/exhaustive\-deps": "warn"
  }
}
.fi
.PP
.SS "Flat Config (eslint\&.config\&.js)"
.PP
.nf
import reactHooks from 'eslint\-plugin\-react\-hooks';

export default [
  {
    files: ['**/*\&.{js,jsx}'],
    plugins: { 'react\-hooks': reactHooks },
    // \&.\&.\&.
    rules: {
      'react\-hooks/rules\-of\-hooks': 'error',
      'react\-hooks/exhaustive\-deps': 'warn',
    }
  },
];
.fi
.PP
.SH "Advanced Configuration"
.PP
\fRexhaustive-deps\fP can be configured to validate dependencies of custom Hooks with the \fRadditionalHooks\fP option\&. This option accepts a regex to match the names of custom Hooks that have dependencies\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
{
  "rules": {
    // \&.\&.\&.
    "react\-hooks/exhaustive\-deps": ["warn", {
      "additionalHooks": "(useMyCustomHook|useMyOtherCustomHook)"
    }]
  }
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
We suggest to use this option \fBvery sparingly, if at all\fP\&. Generally saying, we recommend most custom Hooks to not use the dependencies argument, and instead provide a higher-level API that is more focused around a specific use case\&.
.SH "Valid and Invalid Examples"
.PP
Please refer to the \fRRules of Hooks\fP documentation to learn more about this rule\&.
